  "PROVENANCE": "Sections A, B, C, D, E, F are all measured against the SHIPPED recipe (zero_key_bias_gauge -> zero_value_bias_gauge -> sort_heads -> align_ffn_neurons). Seed coverage is read from the cells, not from the top-level 'seeds' key, which records only the LAST CHUNK'S WINDOW: A at 10 seeds; B at 10 seeds; D at 10 seeds; E at 10 seeds; F at 10 seeds. Section C is a sweep over interpolation level and carries no seed dimension. The floor is 10 seeds and every seeded section meets it. COVERAGE TRADED FOR SEEDS, AND IT CUTS BOTH WAYS: D at eps=1e-06 only; E at eps=1e-06 only; F at eps=1e-06 only. D was 1e-08, 1e-06, 0.0001, E was 1e-08, 1e-06, 0.0001 -- three epsilons at three seeds, until ten seeds at three epsilons proved not to fit the ~600s task-duration cap. Epsilon breadth was spent to buy seed breadth. The reason ten seeds is mandatory is that low-seed numbers in this build kept being overturned by widening. A single epsilon has the MIRROR-IMAGE weakness, and section B demonstrates it directly here: the ruler holds flat across the low decades and then steps off a cliff at eps=0.001, which a section measured at one epsilon could not have seen. Both limits are live and neither substitutes for the other. Big sections were measured in seed windows and merged per seed -- a cell costs about 4.8s -- and every merged cell stores its per-seed values, so min/median/max are computed over the union of chunks rather than over one chunk. READ THE RANGE, NOT THE MEDIAN: at eps=0.001 section B's median is 1.0004 and its worst of 10 seeds is 83.99, with 1 head-order flip(s). Logged as D-3. A median-only report would have shown a flawless ruler. Section R is the retired sort-based recipe and is deliberately NOT re-measured: SORT_ONLY_RECIPE has not changed, so its numbers remain valid for the recipe they describe. Its cells predate per-seed storage and cannot be re-merged."
